        "content": "## PROF1: Professional Behavior — Level 4 / 5 · Proficient\nPediatrics milestone · levels 1–5, novice → expert (Dreyfus)\n\n**Assigned level:** 4 · Proficient — the highest level fully demonstrated on the assessable behaviors; Level 5's community/profession engagement is not assessable in this single encounter.\n\n**Level progression:** Levels 1–4 demonstrated; Level 5 partially demonstrated (modeling within the encounter) with its broader behaviors not assessable in this simulation.\n\n## Evidence by level\n### Level 1 · Novice — Demonstrated\nThe learner identifies expected professional behaviors (honesty, respect, a clear scope of role) and recognizes the trigger for a potential lapse — a worried, hesitant parent influenced by non-evidence sources. The learner opened by naming role and intent clearly.\nLearner said: \"I am Dr. Rissman ... the pediatrics resident taking care of Ava today ... I wanna see, um, what I can help most with today\"\n\n### Level 2 · Advanced beginner — Demonstrated\nProfessional behavior is demonstrated consistently, with no observed lapses, and the learner takes accountability for Ava's care as the responsible pediatrician, offering concrete follow-through.\nLearner said: \"I can write at the end of this, um, call, uh, the exact amount that we can give Ava to protect her from the fevers\"\n\n### Level 3 · Competent — Demonstrated\nThe learner maintains professional composure in an increasingly stressful, pressure-laden situation — a parent repeatedly circling back to the same fear and citing family and online sources — and stays fully engaged rather than deflecting. The learner repeatedly paused to check understanding and invited the parent's concerns back in.\nLearner said: \"I just shared a lot, so I wanna pause and see, um, what questions you might have based on what I shared\"\n\n### Level 4 · Proficient — Demonstrated\nThe learner recognizes the situation (misinformation, family pressure, vaccine hesitancy) as one that could trigger a professionalism lapse — defensiveness, coercion, or dismissiveness — and actively intervenes to prevent it. The learner explicitly framed the professional boundary that the role is to inform, not to coerce, and honored the parent's autonomy while still anchoring to evidence. This reflects a clear sense of duty to the patient.\nLearner said: \"my job is not to convince you one way or another, but just to show you the evidence\"\n\n### Level 5 · Expert — Partially demonstrated / otherwise Not assessable in this simulation (no opportunity)\nWithin the encounter the learner modeled respectful, evidence-based professional behavior. However, Level 5 is defined by coaching others whose behavior falls short and by extending the pediatrician's role into the community, specialty, and profession. A single parent-facing counseling encounter offers no colleague to coach and no venue for community or professional engagement, so these behaviors could not arise here.\n\n## Rationale\nThe learner demonstrated every assessable behavior through Level 4. They identified and named their role honestly (Level 1), behaved professionally throughout with concrete accountability for Ava's follow-up care such as writing out the acetaminophen dose (Level 2), and sustained engagement and composure through a repetitive, emotionally charged exchange without a single lapse (Level 3). Most importantly for Level 4, they recognized a classic professionalism-risk scenario — a hesitant parent pulled by family advice and online groups — and deliberately intervened to keep themselves from sliding into a coercive or dismissive posture, explicitly stating their job was to present evidence rather than to convince, while still clearly advocating for the evidence base. This combination of self-awareness, boundary-setting, and duty to the patient is the defining Level 4 behavior.\n\n## Why not the next level\nLevel 5 turns on coaching others when their behavior fails professional expectations and on extending the pediatrician's role beyond direct patient care into the community, specialty, and profession. Neither behavior could appear in a one-to-one parent counseling encounter: there was no colleague to coach and no community or professional forum in view. The assessment holds at Level 4 because the simulation cannot surface these behaviors (no opportunity), not because the learner fell short.\n\n## Recommendations\nContinue the explicit boundary-naming that distinguishes informing from coercing — it is the hallmark of proficient professional behavior under pressure. One small refinement: at turn 17 the learner conceded not knowing the evidence on delayed schedules; a more polished stance pairs honesty about the limits of recall with a confident statement of what the evidence does show and an offer to follow up, so uncertainty does not read as endorsement of the delay. For example: Suggested wording: \"I don't have a specific study on delaying to four months at my fingertips, and I'll follow up on that for you — but what the large body of evidence does clearly show is that giving these vaccines on this schedule protects her earlier, when she's most vulnerable.\". To build toward Level 5, seek opportunities to model and coach vaccine counseling for junior learners and to engage in community-facing education on immunization.\n\n## Overall impression\n- **Strengths** — Professional, warm, and composed throughout a repetitive and anxiety-laden encounter; named role and intent clearly; took concrete accountability for follow-up care; and explicitly protected against a coercion lapse by framing the role as informing rather than convincing while respecting parental autonomy.\n- **Priority gaps** — None at the assessable levels. The Level 17 concession about not knowing the delayed-schedule evidence risked implying the delay is reasonable; sharper framing would close that gap. Level 5 behaviors (coaching, community/profession engagement) were simply out of scope here.\n- **Top action items**\n  1. Keep explicitly separating informing from coercing when counseling hesitant families.\n  2. When unsure of a specific study, state confidently what the evidence does show and offer to follow up, rather than leaving a vacuum around the non-recommended option.\n  3. Pursue teaching and community-education roles to develop the Level 5 modeling-and-coaching behaviors this encounter could not surface.\n",
